      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n{{answer}}",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/worked-solution": "If we write the integrand as a power of $x$, we have ${x}^{0}=1.$ Therefore, we can use the power rule with $n=0$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ 1+0 & = \\\\ 1\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$",
